sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch09-03> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The one-handed molecular asymmetry of organic products need not be a prerogative of life; it may have started from a non-vital asymmetric force, such as sunlight circularly polarised by reflection from the sea, which acts unequally on right- and left-handed molecules.";
    dc:type "historical-inference";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Biological molecular asymmetry may originate from circularly polarised sunlight";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter IX, “On Concretions, Spicules, and Spicular Skeletons”; topic “Molecular asymmetry and its origin”. Scope: optically active (chiral) compounds produced by living organisms; origin of biological homochirality"
}
